# Never commit build output or credentials.
config.json
server-secret.txt
*.7z
*.zip
*.exe
*.pdb
_dist/
published.json

# Copies of the server's own logs, pulled down by scripts\vps\Get-Logs.ps1 to be
# read. They belong to whichever run made them, not to the repo.
logs/
*.log

# The Discord webhook is a credential: anyone holding the URL can post as the
# bot. Publish-Discord reads it from here on the machine that ships.
bot/news-webhook.txt

# Scratch beside the repo, and the local patch runs that made 1.1.35.
_to_delete/

# Python's bytecode cache. Running the bot once leaves bot\__pycache__
# behind, and it then shows up as untracked in every git status after, which is
# how it nearly went into an unrelated pull request on 2026-09-17.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# What Publish-Discord.ps1 has already announced, one line per channel. Not a
# credential, and not shared either: the two builds announce different
# versions and each shipping machine has its own history, so a tracked copy
# would be a conflict on every release. Ship commits nothing now, but it used
# to commit the whole working tree, which is the other reason this stays out.
bot/announced.json

# Which ticket each person has open, written by the bot beside itself on the
# VPS. It is a list of the Discord ids of everybody waiting on staff, so it
# stays out of the repo if the bot is ever run from a checkout.
bot/open-tickets.json

# Which arrival each person was, written by the bot beside itself on the VPS so
# the number on a join card only ever goes up. A list of Discord ids like the
# one above, and a copy here would be some other run's numbers. The .tmp is the
# half of a write that has not been swapped in yet.
bot/joins.json
bot/joins.json.tmp

# Which faction lead seats are filled, written by the bot beside itself on
# the VPS. Who marked it and when. A copy here would be some other run's.
bot/faction-seats.json
bot/faction-seats.json.tmp

# The bot's heartbeat for the watchdog (#449), rewritten every minute by
# whichever bot is running; a copy here is some other run's.
bot/heartbeat.json
bot/heartbeat.json.tmp

# The dev build's own news webhook, for the dev updates channel. Same rule as
# news-webhook.txt above: anyone holding the URL can post as the bot.
bot/dev-webhook.txt

# Where a certificate lives and how to drive it. Never a password or a key,
# but it names a machine's own signing setup and only that machine needs it.
# scripts\release\Sign-Artifact.ps1 does nothing when this is absent, which is how a
# machine without a certificate still builds. Written from
# records\signing\signing.template.json beside it; in tools\ until 29 Sep.
records/signing/signing.json
# and any copy of it kept beside it while the setup is being changed.
records/signing/signing.json.*

# Microsoft's signing client, Azure.CodeSigning.Dlib.dll and what comes with
# it, fetched from NuGet. records\signing\metadata.json beside it is tracked on
# purpose (records\signing\README.md).
records/signing/client/

# The passphrase the nightly backups are encrypted with. Restore-Backup.ps1
# also looks for it in local\ (ignored below), so the name has to be unable
# to land in here anywhere else. Where it really lives is Documents on
# Patrick's machine and C:\Thornswood-Backup on the VPS, and it must be in both.
thornswood-backup-key.txt
backup-key.txt

# What the voice service makes beside itself the first time it runs. On the VPS
# it lives in its own folder, so these only appear here when somebody runs it
# out of a checkout to try something, which is exactly when nobody is watching
# git status: token-key.txt signs the join tokens, secret.txt is the password
# the account routes check, and owners.json is the register it keeps. Found on
# 23 September by running the service locally to prove a header, which left a
# signing key sitting untracked in voice.
voice/token-key.txt
# Not made by the service: the TURN relay's key, put beside it by hand. Ignored
# here so a copy made to try the relay out of a checkout never reaches git.
voice/turn-key.txt
voice/secret.txt
voice/owners.json
voice/owners.json.tmp
voice/chat/

# Build output, in the repository folder since 25 September rather than in
# C:\Thornswood-Dev (scripts\release\Where.ps1): the packs, the launcher and the
# installer under out\dist, the server payload and thornswood-server.zip.
# A release is what keeps them, on GitHub and the VPS.
/out/

# Things kept beside the repository that never go to GitHub: other servers'
# launcher scripts, a copy of the VPS server settings, backups of old files.
# Everything C:\Thornswood-Dev held that was not the fork or build output.
# Since 29 September also what tools\ held for one machine (issue #440,
# phase 10): the front build thornswood-front.zip, Audit-Mods' mod-audit.json
# and a copy of the backup passphrase. The setup stub is build output, in out\.
/local/
bot/data/
